Refractive Errors (Myopia/Hyperopia/Astigmatism/Presbyopia)
Opthalmology & ENT
Painless blurred vision, Squinting/eyestrain, Improvement with pinhole/lenses

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: OET-039
Reason for Visit: Acute panic and distress due to sudden inability to read medication labels and perceived rapid loss of vision.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient and spouse
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage reported a middle-aged patient experiencing an acute, distressing inability to focus on near objects, specifically resulting in a dangerous medication error tonight. Urgent physical assessment was required to definitively rule out acute cranial nerve palsies (e.g., CN III palsy causing loss of accommodation), acute maculopathy, or impending stroke, and to manage the immediate medication safety risk before routine hours.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: "I suddenly can't read any of the print on my pill bottles. The letters are jumping all over the place, and my eyes feel completely exhausted."
History of Present Illness: Patient reports a progressive, painless difficulty focusing efficiently on near objects or reading fine print, with perfectly preserved distance vision (classic presbyopia). Tonight, while fatigued, the patient experienced a sudden, profound inability to read their medication labels, causing extreme panic regarding impending blindness or stroke. Endorses chronic generalized eye strain and frontal headaches after reading. Relies heavily on holding text further away to gain clarity. Denies any visual field loss, double vision (diplopia), or pain upon eye movement.
Functional Status: Highly distressed, unable to self-administer night-time medications safely.
Social/Safety Concerns: Imminent risk of medication error due to visual inability to read labels.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 140/85 | HR: 88 | Temp: 36.6 | RR: 16 | SpO2: 100% | Wt: 78 Kg
General: Anxious, repeatedly trying and failing to read a small print label held at arm's length.
Physical Exam: Distance visual acuity is 6/6 bilaterally. Near visual acuity (Jaeger chart at 35cm) is markedly reduced to J10. The application of a +2.00 diopter spherical lens (or generic +2.00 reading glasses from the clinician's kit) yields an immediate, objective improvement in near visual acuity back to standard baselines (J1). Pupils are equal, round, and reactive to light and accommodation. No relative afferent pupillary defect. Extraocular movements are perfectly intact without diplopia or ptosis. Confrontation visual fields are full.
Environmental Observations: Dimly lit kitchen; multiple identical-looking medication bottles clustered together.
Medication Reconciliation: Checked against EHR. Patient was about to take a double dose of antihypertensives due to inability to read the label. Corrected by clinician.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Presbyopia with Acute Asthenopia - Status: Exacerbated by fatigue and poor lighting
Differentials:

Third Cranial Nerve (Oculomotor) Palsy - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Normal, symmetric pupillary constriction, absolute absence of ptosis (drooping eyelid), and perfectly intact extraocular motility without any double vision.

Macular Degeneration - Ruled out/unlikely due to: The complete, instant correction of near vision using simple +2.00 magnifying lenses, and the absence of a central scotoma or metamorphopsia on Amsler grid testing.

Acute Stroke - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Full visual fields, normal neurological exam, normal speech, and the isolated nature of the near-focusing deficit.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Moderate (Prevented an acute medication error)

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient presented with acute functional incapacitation and panic secondary to an inability to perform a critical near-vision task (reading medication labels). While the underlying pathology (presbyopia) is chronic and benign, the acute realization of the deficit created a severe immediate safety risk (medication error) and fear of stroke. Urgent, direct clinical evaluation definitively ruled out acute neurological or macular pathology. The diagnostic administration of simple reading glasses instantly reversed the deficit, confirming the loss of crystalline lens elasticity. The visit prevented a dangerous medication overdose, eliminated severe anxiety, and provided an immediate, simple solution.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Directly supervised the correct administration of the patient's nighttime medications. Advised the immediate purchase of +2.00 over-the-counter reading glasses first thing in the morning. Generated a routine referral for a formal optometry review.
Positive Behavioral Change: To permanently eliminate the risk of medication errors, the patient must instantly segregate their morning and evening medications into distinctly different colored, large-compartment weekly pill organizers. By removing the daily requirement to read small, low-contrast print on identical bottles, the patient bypasses the presbyopic deficit entirely, ensuring safe, independent medication compliance (positive reinforcement through restored autonomy).
Home-Based Services (Optional): None.
DME/Safety (Optional): OTC +2.00 Reading glasses.
Follow-up: Patient to see a community Optometrist within the next 2 weeks for formal reading prescription.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Immediate ED presentation if the patient develops sudden double vision, a drooping eyelid, sudden profound loss of vision in one eye, or difficulty speaking/weakness in the limbs.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: Diagnosis is the normal age-related loss of near focusing; requires an optometrist for exact lens prescription.
Clinical Course: Your near vision will not naturally improve. You will require reading glasses permanently for close tasks.
SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

If you suddenly start seeing double, one of your eyelids droops shut, or you lose the ability to speak clearly, call an ambulance immediately.
PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: As we age, the lens inside the eye becomes stiff and loses its ability to flex and focus on objects close to the face. This is a normal, physical change, not a disease or a stroke. Simple magnifying reading glasses will immediately fix the problem.
Actionable Steps: Buy a pair of +2.00 reading glasses from the pharmacy tomorrow. Sort all your medications into a large, weekly pillbox during the day in bright light so you do not have to read small labels at night.

